  "transcript": "# Proposals for backbench debates\n\n## Committee introduces two debate requests\n> Two MPs asked for Chamber debates; both preferred the main Chamber but would accept Westminster Hall if needed.\n\nChair: We will hear two applications for debates in the Chamber or Westminster Hall. First up: Peter Swallow on International Men’s Day.\n\n\n## International Men’s Day: what Peter Swallow asked for\n> Mr Swallow asked for a Chamber debate on 19 November to highlight men's health, education gaps, family roles and paternity leave, but accepted Westminster Hall if necessary.\n\nPeter Swallow: On 19 November the UK joins over 80 countries for International Men’s Day. I want a Commons debate to continue the useful tradition of focusing on issues affecting men and boys.\nPeter Swallow: Key concerns are implementing the men’s health strategy, the mental health crisis among young men and boys, and educational gaps—especially for white working-class boys.\nPeter Swallow: There are positives to discuss too: day one paternity leave started in April, and the Government is reviewing parental leave. We should also discuss how men can help tackle violence against women and girls.\nAlison Hume: We have limited Chamber time. Would you accept Westminster Hall so your debate can be close to 19 November?\nPeter Swallow: I know the pressures. I’d prefer the Chamber, but I understand the Committee’s constraints and would accept Westminster Hall if that’s what you choose.\nChair: Would you want the debate on 19 November itself?\nPeter Swallow: Ideally yes—preferably first debate of the day on 19 November. If that isn’t possible, a Tuesday in Westminster Hall would also work.\nChair: Noted. The Clerks will be in touch.\n\n\n## Safety of frontier AI: what Iqbal Mohamed asked for\n> Mr Mohamed urged urgent, high-profile parliamentary scrutiny of advanced AI risks, suggested pharma-style regulation could help, and said he'd accept Westminster Hall if the Chamber wasn't available.\n\nChair: Next application: Iqbal Mohamed for a Chamber debate on the safety of frontier AI.\nIqbal Mohamed: AI is everywhere. It’s a huge opportunity but also a big risk. I want a Chamber debate so Parliament can act faster than it often does in other areas of policy.\nIqbal Mohamed: The risks are technical, societal and humanitarian. Company leaders have spoken about existential risks—estimates around 10–25%—and Bill Gates has said AI could replace about 40% of jobs.\nIqbal Mohamed: I was the first MP to secure an AI safety debate in Westminster Hall in December 2025; the technology’s pace means we need more attention now to design a regulatory framework that protects people while allowing benefits.\nIqbal Mohamed: From my 25 years in pharmaceuticals, I think principles from pharma regulation—rigorous testing and oversight across the supply chain—could be adapted for AI.\nJonathan Davies: There’s a long waiting list for Chamber time and it can be shortened by urgent statements. Would you accept Westminster Hall if it means getting the debate sooner?\nIqbal Mohamed: Yes—Chamber would give greater public attention, but Westminster Hall is fine if it means the debate happens earlier.\nJonathan Davies: Would you prefer Tuesday or Thursday?\nIqbal Mohamed: Either is fine, though Tuesday is slightly preferable because more MPs are usually present.\nChair: If it’s a Tuesday, which Department would be expected to answer the debate?\nIqbal Mohamed: I believe DCMS now handles digital issues and AI, and there’s an AI Minister attached to the relevant department.\nMartin Vickers: Isn’t it Business—whichever name they’re using this week?\nAlison Hume: DSIT doesn’t exist now.\nIqbal Mohamed: It’s been reorganised and responsibilities are combined, so you might see cross-departmental coverage.\nChair: Thank you. The Clerks will be back in touch about allocation.\nIqbal Mohamed: Thank you. I appreciate the Committee’s time.\n",
